id: mental-model:dave:prenumerical-thinking
title: "Dave / prenumerical thinking"
kind: mental-model
status: draft-0.1
spec_version: mental-model.v0.1
template_ref: templates/mental-model.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave
arena: "Kindergarten math at home with Dave's daughter, before and underneath counting"
updated: 2026-09-09

questions:
  - key: role
    answer: "The person who notices what she already does with how-much before any number word."
  - key: relevance
    answer: >
      Numbers land on top of this. If it is thin, counting to ten is a song
      without meaning. If it is solid, the numbers have somewhere to sit.
  - key: definition
    answer: "Handling how much with no number words: same, different, match, more, less, order, pattern."
  - key: inside
    answer: "Seven moves she can make with her hands."
  - key: show
    answer: "Six household moments, each with one question."
  - key: assumptions
    answer: "What I am taking for granted, marked so I can correct it."
  - key: proof
    answer: "What she does with objects, not what she says about them."
  - key: lacuna
    answer: "Which of these she already has is unknown until I watch."

concept:
  name: Prenumerical thinking
  one_line: "Reasoning about quantity, order, and pattern before the counting words carry meaning."
  inside:
    - name: Sort
      glyph: "● ● | ▲ ▲"
      one_line: "Same and different. This goes with that."
    - name: Match
      glyph: "● – ○"
      one_line: "One cup for each plate."
    - name: Compare
      glyph: "●● ●●●●"
      one_line: "More, less, enough, too many."
    - name: Order
      glyph: "▪ ■ ◼"
      one_line: "Small to big. First, next, last."
    - name: Pattern
      glyph: "● ▲ ● ▲"
      one_line: "What comes next."
    - name: Conserve
      glyph: "●●●  → ● ● ●"
      one_line: "Moved around, still the same pile."
    - name: Quantity words
      glyph: "all / some / none"
      one_line: "All, some, none, more, gone."

show:
  - icon: Sock pile
    do: "Dump the clean socks out."
    say: "Find its match. How did you know?"
  - icon: Table
    do: "Hand her the spoons at dinner."
    say: "One spoon for every bowl. Any left over?"
  - icon: Cups
    do: "Give her three nested cups, then five."
    say: "Line them up, smallest to biggest."
  - icon: Clap
    do: "Clap, clap, stomp. Repeat it twice."
    say: "What comes next?"
  - icon: Two juices
    do: "Pour two different amounts."
    say: "Which has more? Which one do you want?"
  - icon: Blocks in a bag
    do: "Let her feel a bag without looking."
    say: "A lot, or a little?"

embody:
  - "Use the quantity words in ordinary moments. Do not call it a lesson."
  - "Hand her the objects. She does; I narrate what she did."
  - "When she sorts by a rule I did not expect, ask for her rule before offering mine."
  - "Notice what is already there before trying to install anything."

assumptions:
  - text: "These moves come before and underneath counting."
    epistemic_status: assumed
    epistemic_note: "Piaget's ordering. Later work disputes how strict the prerequisite is."
  - text: "She already does most of this in play; my job is to notice, not to teach."
    epistemic_status: assumed
  - text: "Household objects beat printed worksheets at this age."
    epistemic_status: assumed

proof:
  signals:
    - "She matches one-to-one without being told to."
    - "She orders three cups by size, then five."
    - "She continues an AB pattern, then an ABB pattern."
    - "She says 'more' and 'all gone' about the right piles."
  falsifier: "If she can chant to ten but cannot hand one cracker to each person, the words are ahead of the sense."

lacunae:
  - "Which of the seven moves does she already have?"
  - "Must conservation come before counting, or does it grow alongside it?"
  - "Which moments does she start on her own?"

sources:
  - text: "Piaget, The Child's Conception of Number (1952): classification, seriation, conservation."
    epistemic_status: reported
  - text: "Gelman and Gallistel (1978): counting competence earlier than Piaget's prerequisites."
    epistemic_status: disputed
    epistemic_note: "The two sources disagree on ordering; this model keeps both visible."
  - text: "Clements and Sarama, learning trajectories for early math."
    epistemic_status: reported
